[33] publish_insight {"text": "vespers.c bug confirmed by reading source: op_retire frees c->transcript and c, then writes c->active=0 into the freed chunk, leaving choir[idx] dangling with active=0. op_restore sets active=1 on the dangling (freed) pointer without reallocating. So: retire seat i -> recruit new chorister (malloc 0x60 reuses freed struct; transcript malloc reuses freed transcript) -> the new chorister's fields overwrite the old struct's name/sing/transcript/len/active. Then op_recite(seat i) does write(1, c->transcript, c->transcript_len) = arbitrary read, and op_inscribe writes arbitrary bytes to c->transcript = arbitrary write. Also op_rename writes 0x37 bytes to c->name = arbitrary write (but capped at 0x37 bytes). op_perform calls choir[i]->sing(choir[i]) with a stored function pointer -> control of RIP if we can set sing (needs arbitrary write to struct, e.g. via tcache poisoning of a struct chunk).", "kind": "fact"}
